boundary='Chapter 1 remains open. This package audits the required W/V domain mapping using the existing shared Regret API; it adds zero production mathematical results or definitions. Full regret/minimum and no-regret source reconciliation, the logarithmic lower-bound/source claim and other required module audits remain open. There are16 source items but the mandatory proof total is unknown(null). Chapter2 is incomplete; Chapters3–16 are unenumerated and necessary appendices remain required. The total Goal is active. Exact stacked OPEN unmerged PR189 base3e473465298ea1ea10b7771608497d34e558fbeb; no main/live/merge/deploy/retirement claim.'
model='Losses are defined on W, predictions belong to W, and fixed comparators belong to V⊆W. Lean uses X=Subtype W and comparator set {w:Subtype W | w.val∈V}; inclusion embeds a subtype-V comparator without changing its value. The source uses finite-dimensional Euclidean vectors; the shared algebraic API explicitly generalizes the carrier. Footnote1 states a modelling possibility, not a generic algorithm or performance theorem.'
assumptions='Only inclusion V⊆W is needed for the embedding. Finite regret algebra needs no convexity, compactness, probability or nonnegativity. A supplied prediction sequence is shared across every comparator. Its loss-prefix evaluation identity does not prove causal learner recomputation, future independence or existence of a best comparator. A W-loss restricts to V by pullback; a V-only loss is never evaluated outside V.'
parameters='Source round1 is Lean0; sums use range T. At T0 both sums are empty; Lean real division is total. Eventual atTop bounds concern sufficiently large positive horizons. Empty comparator sets give a vacuous property; supplied subtype inhabitants do not prove arbitrary sets are nonempty.'
upper='NoRegret means, for each fixed feasible comparator u and every positive epsilon, normalized regret is eventually at most epsilon. The threshold may depend on u and epsilon. This is an explicit interpretation of the source displayed ordinary-limit inequality: it agrees when the ordinary limit exists, but proves no ordinary limit existence, convergence tozero, absolute bound, nonnegative regret or uniform comparator threshold.'
proof='Subtract the two finite loss sums and distribute subtraction termwise using Mathlib Finset.sum_sub_distrib. For the adapter, tendsto_order makes a comparator-wise bound tending tozero less than any positive epsilon eventually; intersect this event with the supplied actual normalized-regret upper bound and use transitivity. The adapter does not itself construct that bound. The typed illustrative game has V=[0,1],W=[0,2], constant output2 and loss_t(x)=-x onW. For every comparator u∈V, each actual loss gap is u-2≤-1≤0; Finset.sum_nonpos and div_nonpos_of_nonpos_of_nonneg make its finite sum and normalized regret nonpositive. The actual shared adapter applies with bound0. At T2 the same output gives regret -4 against0 and -2 against1. This is a generalized negative-regret test, separate from the source squared guessing game and no universal W performance claim.'
math=r'\begin{aligned}V&\subseteq W,\quad x_t\in W,\quad u\in V,\quad\ell_t:W\to\mathbb R,\\R_T(u)&=\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_t(x_t)-\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_t(u),\\\operatorname{NoRegret}&:\ \forall u\in V,\ \forall\varepsilon>0,\\&\qquad\exists N\ \forall T\ge N:\ R_T(u)/T\le\varepsilon.\end{aligned}'
